 put('accessibility.html','Accessibility checks',intro('Accessible work is easier for everyone to read. Build it in as you go, then check the file you actually share.',tag='Make the work usable')+steps([('Use real headings and clear links','Use the template’s heading styles rather than bold text, and write links that say where they go. Screen readers rely on both to find their way around.'),('Describe images that carry meaning','Give informative images alternative text that explains what they show and why they’re there. Purely decorative images should be marked as decorative so they’re skipped.'),('Caption video and transcribe audio','Add captions to video and offer a text version of any audio, so the content works with the sound off.'),('Make text easy to read','Check contrast: WCAG’s minimum is generally 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Don’t rely on colour alone; add labels, symbols or patterns too.'),('Check the file you share','Accessibility can be lost on export, so check the PDF or published version too: reading order, alternative text, and keyboard access and visible focus for anything interactive.')])+'<section class="reading-section"><h2>The full standard</h2><p>These checks are a starting point, not a claim that the work passes a full accessibility audit. The standard itself has every requirement.</p>'+link_cards([('https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/quickref/','WCAG 2.2 quick reference','Every requirement in the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, with techniques for meeting each one.','book','W3C · w3.org')])+'</section>')
